---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import GuideLayout from '../../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

import shotTransfers from '../../../assets/guide/06-transfer-requests.png';
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="approve-class-change"
  title="Approve a class change request"
  description="Students ask to move class. As the home teacher of the class they are leaving, the decision is yours."
  audience="For teachers"
  readingTime="2 min read"
>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Know where the request came from</h2>
      <p>
        You do not move a student yourself. The <strong>student</strong> raises the request from
        their own app, picking the class they want to move to. It then waits for the home teacher of
        the class they are currently in, which is where you come in.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          <strong>One open request at a time.</strong> A student cannot stack up requests, so there
          is never more than one pending move per student to decide on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Open Transfer Requests</h2>
      <p>
        From <strong>Classrooms</strong>, switch to the <strong>Transfer Requests</strong> tab. The
        count on the tab is how many are waiting on you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each card shows who is asking, the class they are leaving, the class they want, the reason
        they gave, and when they asked.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={shotTransfers}
          alt="The Transfer Requests tab showing one pending request from Rohan Gupta to move from Grade 9 - A to Grade 9 - B, with Approve transfer and Reject buttons."
          loading="eager"
        />
        <figcaption>
          You only see requests to leave a class where you are the home teacher.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Approve or reject</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Approve transfer</strong> moves the student immediately. <strong>Reject</strong>
        asks you for a short note first, which the student sees, so a refusal does not land as
        silence.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          <strong>If a class has no home teacher</strong>, any teacher of that class can decide
          instead. That fallback exists so a request cannot get stuck forever waiting on a role
          nobody holds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>What changes when you approve</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The student joins the new class straight away.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Their counsellor changes with them</strong>, to whoever is assigned to the new
          class. Counsellors are assigned per class, so moving class moves the counsellor.
        </li>
        <li>
          Any open referral stays with the teacher who filed it, and the counsellor working the case
          is told the student has moved.
        </li>
        <li>
          Referrals already filed keep the old class on their record, so the history reads correctly.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="guide-note guide-privacy">
        <p>
          <strong>Think before approving a student mid-case.</strong> If the student has an open
          referral, a move changes who counsels them. That is sometimes exactly right and sometimes
          disruptive. If you are unsure, ask the counselling team before you decide.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-footer">
    <h2>Things worth knowing</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Students can cancel their own request</strong> while it is still pending, so a request
      that disappears before you got to it was most likely withdrawn rather than lost.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>You cannot move a student into a class you do not teach</strong> and you cannot start
      a transfer on a student's behalf. Both are deliberate: the request has to come from the
      student, and the decision has to come from the class they are leaving.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stuck? Email <a href="mailto:admin@zengarden.co.in">admin@zengarden.co.in</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
